In Eq. [ 2 ], the effect of alloying on the plastic shear stress was calculated, where ∆τ is the shear stress, ∆c is the composition; the ratio ∆ τ /∆ c  for each element was estimated from Leslie’s paper. [ 23 ]  The atomic percentage (at. pct) of each element was approximated from SEM-EDS analyses on the ferrite for each steel (the specific compositions are in Table  IV ). The increase in yield strength due to solid-solution strengthening (Δ σ ) was calculated using Eq. [ 3 ], where it is twice the increase in shear stress; the increase in ferrite hardness due to solid-solution strengthening (ΔHV) is the change in yield strength divided by 3.3 as shown in Eq. [ 4 ]. [ 24 ]  In addition, the intrinsic iron hardness (60 HV) was added to the final predicted hardness values from Leslie. [ 24 ]  Hall–Petch calculations were also carried out for all compositions where k is 6.5 MPa mm 1/2  (the specific grain sizes used for the calculations are in Table  V ). [ 25 ]  From the calculations, the conversion from yield strength to hardness equation is as shown in Eq. [ 5 ] [ 26 ]  as follows: 5 Y S = - 90.7 + 2.876 H v . \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
